<header><p class="enc-kicker">Person</p><h1 class="enc-title">Francesco Guicciardini</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates"><a href="/years/1483">1483</a>–<a href="/years/1540">1540</a></span> · <a href="/centuries/16th-century">16th century</a></p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Francesco Guicciardini (1483–1540) was a Florentine historian, diplomat and statesman. He served as ambassador to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/spain">Spain</a>, as papal governor of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/modena">Modena</a>, Reggio and the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/romagna">Romagna</a> and as an adviser to the first Medici duke, and he was a friend and critic of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/niccolo-machiavelli">Machiavelli</a>. His History of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/italy">Italy</a>, covering 1490 to 1534, is considered a founding work of modern historiography for its use of documents and its analysis of political motives.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/francesco-guicciardini.jpg" alt="Francesco Guicciardini" width="320" height="421" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Cristofano dell'Altissimo · Public domain · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Ritratto_di_Francesco_Guicciardini.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Early life and background</h2><p>Francesco Guicciardini was born in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/florence">Florence</a> on 6 March 1483, the third of eleven children of Piero di Iacopo Guicciardini. The family belonged to the Florentine oligarchy and supported the Medici. His godfather was the philosopher <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/marsilio-ficino">Marsilio Ficino</a>, and he received a humanist education before studying law at the Universities of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ferrara">Ferrara</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/padua">Padua</a> until 1505. At twenty-three he was appointed by the Signoria to teach legal studies at the Florentine Studio. In 1508 he married Maria Salviati, daughter of Alamanno Salviati, and in the same year wrote a family memoir and began his Ricordi, a collection of personal maxims.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Career</h2><p>In 1512 the Signoria sent him as ambassador to Ferdinand the Catholic of Aragon. He came to regard Ferdinand as a model of political deceit. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/pope-leo-x">Pope Leo X</a> made him governor of Reggio in 1516 and of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/modena">Modena</a> in 1517, and in 1523 Clement VII appointed him viceregent of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/romagna">Romagna</a>.</p><p>He urged Clement to join the League of Cognac in 1526 against <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/charles-v-holy-roman-emperor">Charles V</a>, and was made lieutenant-general of the papal army, though he could not move its commander to act. The <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sack-of-rome-1527">Sack of Rome</a> followed in 1527. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/florence">Florence</a>'s new republic declared him a rebel in March 1530. After the city fell to imperial troops, he was charged with punishing those who had resisted the Medici, and Benedetto Varchi called him more cruel than the others.</p><p>He governed <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bologna">Bologna</a> from 1531 and later advised <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/alessandro-de-medici">Alessandro de' Medici</a>, defending him before <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/charles-v-holy-roman-emperor">Charles V</a> at <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/naples">Naples</a> in 1535. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/cosimo-de-medici">Cosimo de' Medici</a> dismissed him soon after taking power, and he retired to his villa at <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/arcetri">Arcetri</a>, where he died in 1540 without male heirs.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Works and ideas</h2><p>None of his works was published in his lifetime. The first sixteen of the twenty books of his History of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/italy">Italy</a> appeared in 1561, and the first English translation in 1579. The Ricordi, about 220 maxims, and his other writings came to light only after his descendants opened the family archives in 1857. He wrote the History in his last years, drawing on government records and his own experience, and he traced events to the self-interest of the emperors, princes and popes in command.</p><p>He corresponded with <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/niccolo-machiavelli">Niccolò Machiavelli</a> until the latter's death in 1527, but objected that Machiavelli put things too absolutely, and he did not agree that <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/rome">Rome</a> could serve as a perfect norm. Like many Florentine aristocrats he favoured a mixed republican government on the Venetian model, though he worked closely with the Medici.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Francesco Guicciardini” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>The republic’s crises also produced modern political thought: Niccolò Machiavelli’s The Prince and Discourses on Livy and <b>Francesco Guicciardini</b>’s History of Italy grew out of the experience of its fall.</blockquote><a href="/empire/republic-of-florence">Republic of Florence · Global &amp; Regional Impact</a></li></ul></section>
<section class="enc-where enc-born"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Born and died</h2><p class="enc-born-p">Born in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/florence">Florence</a>, in what is now <a href="/countries/italy">Italy</a>. Died in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/arcetri">Arcetri</a>, in what is now <a href="/countries/italy">Italy</a>. <span class="enc-born-src">Places as Wikidata records them.</span></p><figure class="st-locator enc-loc"><a href="/atlas?at=43.77,11.25&amp;year=1520" aria-label="Florence on the atlas"><svg class="map map-globe map-loc" viewBox="0 0 10000 10000" role="img" aria-label="Globe: the birthplace of Francesco Guicciardini"><image href="/maps/globes/g000n30.svg" x="0" y="0" width="10000" height="10000"/><path class="map-dot-halo" d="M5691 3800h0"/><path class="map-dot-me" d="M5691 3800h0"/></svg></a><figcaption>The dot marks the birthplace. <a href="/atlas?at=43.77,11.25&amp;year=1520">What was here in 1520? &rarr;</a></figcaption></figure></section>
